# SOC Metrics and Objectives

## Summary
SOC teams use specific metrics to measure their 
effectiveness and response capabilities. These metrics 
help organizations understand how well their security 
team is performing and where improvements are needed.

## Key Concepts
- Service Level Agreement (SLA) — A document signed 
  between the internal SOC team and company management 
  that defines expected response times and performance 
  standards
- MTTD — Mean Time to Detect. How long it takes the 
  SOC to detect a threat after it occurs
- MTTA — Mean Time to Acknowledge. How long it takes 
  the SOC to acknowledge and begin responding to an 
  alert after it is detected
- MTTR — Mean Time to Respond. How long it takes the 
  SOC to fully contain and resolve a threat
- False Positive — An alert that triggers but turns out 
  to not be an actual threat
- True Positive — An alert that triggers and is confirmed 
  to be a real threat

## How SOC Teams Are Measured
- Lower MTTD means threats are caught faster
- Lower MTTA means analysts are responding quicker
- Lower MTTR means incidents are resolved more efficiently
- SLA defines the acceptable thresholds for each metric

## How Defenders Use These Metrics
- Identifying gaps in detection and response processes
- Justifying budget and staffing to management
- Measuring improvement over time
- Holding the SOC team accountable to agreed standards

## My Key Takeaway
SOC metrics exist to make security measurable. A SOC 
team that cannot measure its performance cannot improve it.

